your SaaS products? And Intercom's by far the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:43.840
most. I know people constantly complain about&nbsp;
just how unclear it was and how high it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:48.560
and now you guys are at the forefront of how to&nbsp;
price AI products. So, we're going to get to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:53.200
but just talk about the lessons and what&nbsp;
happened there with pricing back in the day.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:17:00.640
Yeah, so I want to just validate your survey&nbsp;
data. Yes, people abhorred our pricing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:08.320
It was a meme. There were actual funny popular&nbsp;
viral memes on Twitter that were making fun of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:16.400
our pricing. Part of the problem, if not all of&nbsp;
the problem, well, there's two problems. One was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:21.840
our strategy, super unfocused. As you said, we're&nbsp;
trying to do all the things for all the people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:25.040
and when you're trying to do all&nbsp;
the things for all the people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:34.400
your efforts to capture all that different types&nbsp;
of value are going to necessitate pretty complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:39.280
pricing. If you're like customer service&nbsp;
and you're selling seats and you're doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:43.760
outbound messaging and you need to charge for&nbsp;
messages and you're doing like SD or Messenger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:49.760
on a website and you need to charge for leads,&nbsp;
already that's just metrics in every direction.&nbsp;

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:54.320
And then if you're trying to sell to many&nbsp;
different sizes of customers, you need tiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.320 --> 00:18:01.120
and gates and it just became a behemoth. So,&nbsp;
part of the problem was the unfocused strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:08.160
and then the other part of the problem was an&nbsp;
unwillingness to frankly make bold decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:14.400
say no, pick a lane and actually take pain&nbsp;
in the short term for the long term. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:18.800
rolled out this new pricing, and this is even&nbsp;
before the Fin pricing you're talking about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:24.320
When I came back and I said, "Yes, we're going to&nbsp;
lose a lot of revenue here." I can't remember how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:32.640
much we wrote down, but we actually have already&nbsp;
given away something like $50 million in ARR.&nbsp;

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:38.880
We've reduced the prices for a lot of customers&nbsp;
just to give them way simpler pricing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:43.920
surprise, surprise, when people feel like&nbsp;
they have far simpler, more predictable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:52.240
fairer pricing, they'll stick around longer and&nbsp;
it creates so much more ease in the company and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:57.680
promotes a healthier relationship with the&nbsp;
customer too. When our people saw that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:04.560
were screwing customers effectively in every&nbsp;
direction, it starts to erode the idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:08.240
we care about our customers and then they&nbsp;
make other customer-unfriendly decisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:11.920
And so one of the values I promoted when I came&nbsp;
back was that we would be customer-obsessed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:16.160
and so we had to kill our old pricing&nbsp;
and give away a lot of revenue. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:22.400
that was the spirit behind the changes. But we can&nbsp;
talk about the Fin pricing if you want to also.

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:26.080
Let's save that because that's a really&nbsp;
important topic that I think people need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:32.240
to hear. Let's talk about the shift and how you&nbsp;
made this actually happen. You make it sound like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:37.040
oh, not fully, but it's oh, we have to&nbsp;
do, it wasn't working anyway. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:41.760
no risk to go all in on this AI thing.&nbsp;
You're making $150 million a year ARR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:45.360
you're worth at least a billion dollars&nbsp;
at that point as a business. Yeah.

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:46.440
I mean multiple billions.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:46.720
Multiple billions.

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:50.720
We were making more money than that, so we&nbsp;
were like multiple hundreds of millions.

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:55.360
Yeah, okay. Very difficult to actually&nbsp;
do even if things don't feel like they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:58.480
growing anymore. So, first of all, just&nbsp;
what was the moment, if there was one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:03.760
of just like, okay, the six-week experiment&nbsp;
of someone building a Fin internally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:07.680
was that being like, this is it, or was there&nbsp;
another moment of like, let's go all in on this?

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:16.960
It was the combination of the company being older,&nbsp;
us all, me and the founders being impatient like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:20.320
are we going to make something out of&nbsp;
this? We went through a time when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:25.120
company was worth a lot. We're private&nbsp;
so we don't have a daily mark to market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:33.520
but all the other public software companies&nbsp;
dropped 80%, 85, 90%. We saw our revenue growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:41.680
crater. We were used to nice double digits. We&nbsp;
were in low single digits. And so part of it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:50.720
let's do something here. Another part of it was my&nbsp;
own anger and dissatisfaction with how the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:56.800
was being run and the mistakes that I made myself.&nbsp;
I made a lot of compromises as a lot of founders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:09.600
and founding CEOs do to placate employees or do&nbsp;
it out fear to bring investors along, following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:17.680
advice in the industry and best practices.
You betray your intuition in little bits and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:24.080
pieces over the years when the bright spark&nbsp;
of your original idea turns into this big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:31.040
unstoppable, scary corporate beast and a little&nbsp;
bit of you dies every single time you go and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:40.640
betray yourself in that way. If you could pick&nbsp;
in your mind three or four tech darlings from 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:47.840
years ago when you meet the CEO and talk to them&nbsp;
privately, very few of them feel outstanding about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:52.880
the state of their culture and the decisions that&nbsp;
they make and the way in which they have to work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:59.600
All of them have betrayed themselves in little&nbsp;
ways, and I had left the business, I was super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:05.600
sick, I was burned out frankly from the revenue&nbsp;
even having started to slow down before I left.&nbsp;

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:13.360
I had been attacked unfairly in the press, just&nbsp;
all of me was just fed up and I decided to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:21.520
a very authoritarian, top-down, aggressive&nbsp;
founder-first approach to all the things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:31.200
and I found that deeply cathartic and that was&nbsp;
the thing that led to me in part. The other was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:37.120
just good old-fashioned logic and the other was&nbsp;
desperation saying, we're doing the AI thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:44.240
the AI thing, exciting and sexy. We need some&nbsp;
new energy thing here. The new AI thing makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:52.640
sense. And also just my intuition says, go&nbsp;
for it. And so when people tell these stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:59.600
they rewrite history in their minds for&nbsp;
the stories to be elegant and also so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:04.800
that they support their own self-aggrandized&nbsp;
narratives about their brilliance. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:10.000
it's a big messy cocktail of things. And anyway,&nbsp;
that's my attempt at explaining the cocktail.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:15.200
I saw a stat that when you first launched,&nbsp;
when you first had this kind of prototype,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:19.760
you were losing money on every transaction&nbsp;
that you're charging like a dollar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:23.735
it cost you $120, something like that.

00:23:23.735 --> 00:23:24.640
That's right. 120 cents. Yeah, yeah.

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:29.280
120 cents. Okay. So, there's&nbsp;
a lot of vision here if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:33.200
is going to get to a place where this&nbsp;
actually will be great and affordable.

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:42.400
It's really funny. We charge 99 cents to resolve&nbsp;
tickets, customer problems, and we have a higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:48.880
resolution rate than anyone else, and we are proud&nbsp;
of that and we obsess over that. It is the metric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:58.400
by which these agents are assessed, and we wanted&nbsp;
our revenue to be 100% aligned with the value that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:04.560
they attained because we had all this scar tissue&nbsp;
from pricing prior that felt unfair to customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:09.840
So, we said, what's the most fair that we can&nbsp;
possibly find? Now, when we did all our research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:16.880
we found that many SaaS businesses were spending&nbsp;
between 20 and $30 per ticket resolved. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:25.600
were spending 22. Now, consumer businesses,&nbsp;
maybe they go down to $5. We were thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:32.640
can we charge $10? That seems fair. It's half&nbsp;
price. Can we charge $5? Can we even charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:40.720
two and a half dollars? But early on we started to&nbsp;
sense that people just wouldn't value the digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:46.080
work as much as the human work, even though&nbsp;
the digital work is better, more consistent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:52.640
always available, makes the customer far happier.
And so we actually started to lean into a price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.640 --> 00:25:00.480
that we thought would be was the nexus between us&nbsp;
earning the most and it being the most palatable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.480 --> 00:25:09.680
We basically said that if someone is not prepared&nbsp;
to pay 99 cent for us to rapidly and elegantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:15.920
perfectly and excellently solve their customer's&nbsp;
problem, we need to wrap this up. We don't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.920 --> 00:25:23.120
business here. So, that was where the 99 cent came&nbsp;
from. I always believe that that pricing should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:28.800
come from value and not from costs. The cost is&nbsp;
our problem. We just had this sense and intuition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:36.240
early on that this thing will get cheaper and it&nbsp;
got a lot cheaper. The margin moves around, but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:43.920
make a margin that makes this more than worth our&nbsp;
while, and we know our customers get an excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:50.800
deal and are able to deliver to their customers&nbsp;
a level of service that they never could before.

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:56.160
It's a very clear pitch. We just had&nbsp;
Madhavan the podcast and the pricing expert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.160 --> 00:26:00.320
and he has this phrase, beautifully simple&nbsp;
pricing is where you want to get to. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:03.040
he's a huge fan of outcome-based pricing,&nbsp;
which is what you're describing here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:07.680
where you pay for an outcome. So, you guys are&nbsp;
in the magic quadrant of his pricing advice.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:10.720
Yes, thank God our pricing wars are over.

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:11.920
Finally.

00:26:11.920 --> 00:26:12.480
Yeah.

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.040
Okay, so going back to how you&nbsp;
actually did this thing. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:21.040
basically you described what many people think of&nbsp;
now as founder mode, just top down, as you said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:24.640
the third area, and just here's what we're&nbsp;
doing. We're not going to sit around waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:30.640
for you to give me ideas. What did you&nbsp;
do? What did that look like internally?

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:36.000
There was a couple of things. One was we&nbsp;
were burning a lot of money, so I cut a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:41.120
lot of costs aggressively. Canned a bunch of&nbsp;
different projects. We had this big glorious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:46.720
office we were about to fit out and I'm like,&nbsp;
we're about to hit negative growth territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:53.040
stop it. And a lot of companies were really&nbsp;
stuck in the prior world where they just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:57.920
were used to being super successful, rich and&nbsp;
wealthy and spent like drunken sailors. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.920 --> 00:27:03.040
I stopped all of that, got really frugal in&nbsp;
ways I never thought I would. I still haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:07.920
touched the interior design of this office. I'm&nbsp;
in here, even though I call it the hotel Marriott,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:15.200
I'm sick of it. Anyway, that was one. Another&nbsp;
was picked a lane. Strategically we were all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:20.320
over the place and I said, "We're doing service."&nbsp;
Zendesk had been acquired a couple years prior.&nbsp;

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:25.120
They were strategically, energetically, culturally&nbsp;
dead. They were upsetting customers in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:28.720
market. There's an opportunity there. We're&nbsp;
doing service, forget all the other stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:34.000
even though there was a lot of people in the&nbsp;
company saying, well, shit, we still have $80&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:40.080
million of ARR that we're getting from the other&nbsp;
thing and we're really good at that, and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:45.840
a big opportunity. There's other companies in this&nbsp;
space worth billions. It was the type of decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:52.000
that where I had to practice the professional&nbsp;
CEO approach, which is, "Hey folks, what do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:56.480
all think? Let's take everyone's input. Let's&nbsp;
put it all down on a spreadsheet." Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.480 --> 00:28:03.120
had color beside all of the different options that&nbsp;
we may take. Let's make a group decision. I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:07.360
"Sorry, this is what we're doing." So,&nbsp;
I was very dictatorial in that respect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:12.240
We had no one making decisions, so somebody&nbsp;
needed to, even if I had some qualms about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:16.320
the decisions myself, I couldn't predict&nbsp;
the future, but someone had to make a call.&nbsp;

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:21.280
Obviously as soon as AI came around, I jumped&nbsp;
hard on AI and announced that we were going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:25.680
spend nearly $100 million of our own cash&nbsp;
on that. We allocated a lot of capital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:29.600
but I also restarted the culture. We had just a&nbsp;
very comfortable culture as a lot of companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:35.760
did. There was a lot of focus on social issues&nbsp;
and a lot of complaining and dissatisfaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:41.760
and I rewrote the values designed to be a sharp&nbsp;
knife to cut out the parts of the company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:46.080
I just knew wouldn't be effective. So,&nbsp;
I said that people must be resilient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:52.240
that we had very high standards, that we'd work&nbsp;
incredibly hard, that shareholder value was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:56.160
most important thing that we'd optimize for. A&nbsp;
lot of things that were controversial for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.160 --> 00:29:02.400
prior crowd. And then I designed these quarterly&nbsp;
performance processes where not only would you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:08.080
a mark or a grade for your performance against&nbsp;
your goals that quarter, but you'd also get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:14.240
score for your behavior against the values.
And I hard coded a formula myself, and so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:19.600
took it out of the manager's hands to say, if&nbsp;
people got below a certain mark, respectfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:23.600
and lovingly, we would say, thank you for your&nbsp;
service. We're going to go forward without you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:29.120
And so you do that just a small number of quarters&nbsp;
and you can start to shape an organization that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:33.680
design and the image of the values you want to&nbsp;
create. And obviously there was a lot of pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:38.720
a lot of satisfaction. There was attempts at&nbsp;
soft coup, there was letters sent to the board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:51.360
people really unhappy, but on the other side&nbsp;
of it, the people left were the most incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:57.520
entrepreneurial, brave, inspiring, happy&nbsp;
individuals you could possibly imagine. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.520 --> 00:30:07.520
then you hire in their image. We ran an anonymous&nbsp;
employee survey, I think 15 or 16 months after I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.520 --> 00:30:11.760
started aggressively working through the org and&nbsp;
rebuilding the org and rebuilding the culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:19.680
and we had a 98 to 99% approval of&nbsp;
management, leadership and new strategy.&nbsp;

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:26.160
And this is coming from me having the lowest&nbsp;
Glassdoor rating for a CEO I had ever seen when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:35.200
I came back. So, I just want to explain that being&nbsp;
that deliberate about your culture and upsetting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:41.920
lot of people is the path through which you can&nbsp;
create a culture where people are super happy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:48.560
super engaged, super aligned, and now we have&nbsp;
just this highly performant organization. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:53.520
we're messy in many ways. So, that was a big&nbsp;
part of it too. So, it was kind of strategically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:59.680
picking a lane. Remaking how we go to market,&nbsp;
the pricing was a really, really big piece that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:08.400
had a big effect. Betting on AI and then culture.&nbsp;
And I kind of buried the AI thing because frankly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:14.000
none of this would matter if we didn't bet on AI.&nbsp;
So, the story could all be summed up by saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:19.440
when you ask what did I do, it was that&nbsp;
we built Fin and that changed everything.

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:21.760
You said that this was very unpopular. I imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:25.520
many people were not happy with all&nbsp;
the change and how top-down this was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:29.920
you said there was a soft coup. Is there more you&nbsp;
could share about that? I never heard that story.

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:37.760
When you make that degree of change and you&nbsp;
tell people that they're in control like we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:42.160
did in the previous generation of late-stage&nbsp;
businesses, there's going to be some friction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:49.840
when you change the rules. And it's my strong&nbsp;
belief that great employees and great companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:57.840
want and are constructed out of a very clear and&nbsp;
strong hierarchy where it is the responsibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:05.760
of the CEO to make brave and hard decisions&nbsp;
unilaterally, yes, using their experts as inputs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:12.320
and be responsible for the outcome. If I make&nbsp;
decisions that propel the company in the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:18.400
thankfully my decisions have, I get rewards and&nbsp;
kudos and I get to go back to the board and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:24.320
I want a bigger grant. If I don't, I get&nbsp;
fired and I should get fired. If my big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:30.080
brave, unilateral decisions put us in the toilet,&nbsp;
then I have to take responsibility for that also.&nbsp;

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:36.080
So, that's how in my humble opinion, it should&nbsp;
work. And I, for one, don't know of a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:41.600
company that doesn't work that way. You'll see&nbsp;
from time to time, I did this a couple of years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:46.960
ago, people will construct these indexes of the&nbsp;
performance of companies that are founder led,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:52.000
and of course this is a self-serving statement,&nbsp;
but it's also true. And surprise, surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:58.080
the founder led companies perform substantially&nbsp;
better because they have the moral authority and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:02.880
the willingness to take the risks that the&nbsp;
professional CEOs don't have the remit for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:08.880
The professional CEOs are typically told, don't&nbsp;
mess things up, and the founders are bored if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:14.800
they're not taking the risk of messing things up&nbsp;
from time to time. And so that's in my opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:19.280
what creates greatness and great innovation.&nbsp;
But like I said, there will be friction&nbsp;&nbsp;

