00:51:54.560 --> 00:51:59.920
We and I will be nothing if we didn't&nbsp;
have actual AI scientists and leaders.&nbsp;

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:06.080
It's the only way we can be successful here.&nbsp;
We have an incredible person who by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:06.080 --> 00:52:11.440
this is out will have received a promotion to&nbsp;
chief AI officer. I keep announcing all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:11.440 --> 00:52:17.360
things and that's great confidence to you.&nbsp;
Fergal Reid, and he's just one of the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:17.360 --> 00:52:22.320
best in AI applications, and we happened&nbsp;
to be working with him for many years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:22.320 --> 00:52:28.400
So, part of it is finding the talent and part of&nbsp;
it is bringing in the young talent too. AI is kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.400 --> 00:52:35.360
of a young man's game, and I'm young, but I'm not&nbsp;
as young as a lot of the kids building AI. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.360 --> 00:52:42.720
learning to empower and enable them and learn from&nbsp;
them too is a really big deal. And unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:47.040
part of what you learn from them is the only way&nbsp;
you're going to win right now is if you work your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:47.040 --> 00:52:54.640
ass off, because all these little AI companies&nbsp;
run by kids in their twenties are literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.640 --> 00:53:05.200
working 12 hours a day, literally 365 days a year.
No joke, all of them. And that's not a fun idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:05.200 --> 00:53:11.360
for many of us, especially those who've grown&nbsp;
up. Some people in our generation have kids or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:11.360 --> 00:53:16.400
a lot of them do. There's comfort and stability&nbsp;
in your life. You don't want to work like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:16.400 --> 00:53:25.280
but if you want in, that's part of the price&nbsp;
and that's how so many of these young new AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.280 --> 00:53:30.640
are going to win because very few of the previous&nbsp;
generation companies are willing to make all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.640 --> 00:53:38.000
those changes and go all the way in. And so my&nbsp;
actual advice, which is not that helpful, is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:44.480
if founders of previous generation companies are&nbsp;
themselves not willing to roll up their sleeves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:44.480 --> 00:53:52.960
and get into it and work as hard as the kids,&nbsp;
hire a kid. You can be a chairperson like I was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:52.960 --> 00:53:58.240
have a lot of fun. You can mentor the kid, hire&nbsp;
a kid because you're in the wrong job, buddy.

00:53:58.240 --> 00:54:02.160
I love how pragmatic this advice is and what's&nbsp;
interesting as you talk about 12 hours a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:02.160 --> 00:54:06.160
every day, it's like we're trying to&nbsp;
get close to what agents are doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:08.800
which is half, that's basically 50% of agents.

00:54:08.800 --> 00:54:17.200
But that's not just a poetic cute thing to&nbsp;
say, that comes from something very real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:23.840
which is these younger companies know how&nbsp;
to use AI in ways that the older companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:23.840 --> 00:54:30.560
don't. The younger companies are vibe coding&nbsp;
and using AI for their creative work and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.560 --> 00:54:36.480
their job descriptions. I guarantee you go to&nbsp;
companies of our generation and even we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:36.480 --> 00:54:42.960
had to push people, you go to companies of our&nbsp;
generation, most people in most organizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:42.960 --> 00:54:48.960
particularly non-technical organizations, they're&nbsp;
not using any AI. Maybe they're starting to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:48.960 --> 00:54:55.440
ChatGPT to write a job description, but&nbsp;
they're not doing it by default. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:55.440 --> 00:55:02.320
that's more than a joke. You're competing&nbsp;
with young companies that are in part AI.

00:55:02.320 --> 00:55:07.200
This reminds me, I did an interview with the&nbsp;
Perplexity founders. It was, I just checked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:12.240
April 2024, so just over a year ago. And they&nbsp;
were saying that the way they operated, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.240 --> 00:55:17.280
sounded it's so crazy at the time, is anytime they&nbsp;
had a question for anyone else on the team, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.280 --> 00:55:23.200
first asked ChatGPT about it, and then they go ask&nbsp;
the person as like, that is insane. And now this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.200 --> 00:55:27.040
is just obvious. That's what we all do now.&nbsp;
Just like, hey, I'm just going to talk voice.

00:55:28.720 --> 00:55:35.120
It's a prime example. They're doing many&nbsp;
such things. When I say 365 days a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:35.120 --> 00:55:40.240
they're the company I think of because they're&nbsp;
doing exactly that. All these young companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:40.240 --> 00:55:47.680
doing wild, weird and ridiculous things that&nbsp;
people you are in my age kind of chuckle at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:53.920
but it's business as usual for them. So,&nbsp;
there's just a big mind shift, cultural shift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:53.920 --> 00:56:00.160
and there's a culture clash of the previous&nbsp;
generation versus the new generation. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:00.160 --> 00:56:04.640
sooner you kind of wrap your head around that, the&nbsp;
sooner you can start to unstake yourself, I think.

00:56:04.640 --> 00:56:11.280
And just to build on that, the sounds crazy&nbsp;
to work this hard, it sounds very stressful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.280 --> 00:56:15.920
not fun. Why would I do this? This sucks.&nbsp;
But at the same time, this is, as you said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:15.920 --> 00:56:20.480
such an unusual rare opportunity.&nbsp;
There's so much opportunity. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:20.480 --> 00:56:23.680
so much wealth being created. There's so many&nbsp;
businesses being created. This is the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:23.680 --> 00:56:26.880
if you were to ever work really&nbsp;
hard, this is a good time to do it.

00:56:26.880 --> 00:56:35.360
I think so. I don't actually generally promote&nbsp;
working that hard. I try to not fetishize it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:35.360 --> 00:56:42.880
I actually think a life well-lived includes&nbsp;
taking slow walks in nature where you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:42.880 --> 00:56:49.840
thinking about ARR growth or hiring your chief&nbsp;
revenue officer, not going to eight meetings a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:54.160
day. Maybe you should go to no meetings a&nbsp;
day, certainly not working 12 hours a day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:54.160 --> 00:57:02.000
I don't actually promote that in general&nbsp;
as a thing one should do with their life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:02.640 --> 00:57:12.320
I'm simply saying that if you want to compete&nbsp;
and enjoy success in this age, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:12.320 --> 00:57:21.360
you need to be doing AI, that is the price.
So, you either decide to pay the price or get out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:21.360 --> 00:57:26.960
Don't half-ass it. You see all these companies&nbsp;
saying, we do AI and they've just sprinkle a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:26.960 --> 00:57:33.040
little bit of crappy AI and they've got the same&nbsp;
cultures. It won't work. The one thing I will say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:33.040 --> 00:57:39.360
the one little asterisk to my first point is&nbsp;
that all great people and great things have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:47.040
been achieved through hard work. And so I'm&nbsp;
speaking out of both sides of my mouth here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:47.040 --> 00:57:54.640
to younger people to let them know that every way&nbsp;
of living is valid, but people who have achieved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:54.640 --> 00:58:01.200
things have always worked hard and they find a way&nbsp;
to enjoy it too. And particularly in 2025 in AI.

00:58:01.200 --> 00:58:03.600
I want to follow this thread. I was going&nbsp;
to ask you this earlier, but I didn't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:03.600 --> 00:58:08.880
and I want to see if this takes us somewhere&nbsp;
interesting, just watching you speak and talk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.880 --> 00:58:15.440
You're very self-reflective, very centered.&nbsp;
You have these really good breaths you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:15.440 --> 00:58:19.600
when you think about something. I met you a&nbsp;
long time ago, randomly at a party when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:19.600 --> 00:58:24.160
were just starting Intercom. I don't think you&nbsp;
were like that. During this kind of two-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:24.160 --> 00:58:28.160
period was there kind a transformation that&nbsp;
you went through to kind of become this?

00:58:28.160 --> 00:58:31.840
Absolutely. Yeah. There's a couple things.&nbsp;
First, I mean, there's three things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:31.840 --> 00:58:40.240
come to mind. Working in a startup for 14&nbsp;
years has a certain way of kicking you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:49.600
the head many times a day that either kills you&nbsp;
or makes you far stronger. So, that's one piece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.600 --> 00:58:55.360
There's no elegance to that point, but I think&nbsp;
we can all intuit that that level of experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:55.360 --> 00:59:04.240
teaches you something, you grow up very fast.&nbsp;
Point two is I did a lot of therapy. I found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:09.120
this amazing guy 12 years ago. He started a&nbsp;
couple of his own tech companies and talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:09.120 --> 00:59:15.440
in public. He only coached and was a therapist&nbsp;
to CEOs. He's now kind of in a later stage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:15.440 --> 00:59:22.720
his career. But this amazing guy, his name is&nbsp;
Yosi Amram, amazing guy. I just landed on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:22.720 --> 00:59:33.600
feet. I just didn't know who I was dealing with.
But one of the greatest minds and teachers of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:33.600 --> 00:59:38.560
last, I don't know, many decades, people don't&nbsp;
even know him, but he's taught and worked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:38.560 --> 00:59:48.160
many CEOs and he just helped me get to know me and&nbsp;
take time for myself. And people like to hate on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:48.160 --> 00:59:55.120
therapy right now. I think a lot of therapy sucks&nbsp;
and a lot of therapists are not good. And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:55.120 --> 01:00:06.160
fear that actually therapy will lobotomize them&nbsp;
and turn them into thumb sucking, navel-gazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:06.880 --> 01:00:15.200
soft, irrelevant losers that won't have that&nbsp;
edge anymore. And the interesting thing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:15.200 --> 01:00:24.080
12 years of weekly therapy and spiritual work&nbsp;
is that it takes your edges off, but they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:24.080 --> 01:00:28.160
all edges that are super counterproductive.&nbsp;
All the edges that made you an asshole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:28.160 --> 01:00:36.240
got you triggered, miscommunicated or fought&nbsp;
back when you were insecure, they take all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:42.560
the edges away then help you see yourself and&nbsp;
love yourself so much more for who you are.&nbsp;

01:00:42.560 --> 01:00:46.640
Be completely unafraid to acknowledge the&nbsp;
things you're not good at, but own the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:46.640 --> 01:00:52.320
you are. And in understanding yourself, you&nbsp;
understand others better and can communicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:52.320 --> 01:01:00.320
in a substantially more connected and authentic&nbsp;
way. Great, great therapy and it has to be great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:00.320 --> 01:01:10.080
is a recipe for brilliant leadership in my&nbsp;
opinion. And then the third part is two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:10.080 --> 01:01:17.120
away where I ran away, where I was sick, revenue&nbsp;
growth wasn't doing so hot. I unsuccessfully tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:17.120 --> 01:01:24.240
to defend myself from a bunch of fake bullshit&nbsp;
in the newspapers. I mean I was beat up. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:24.240 --> 01:01:33.280
in a moment like that, your ego, any sense&nbsp;
you have of your greatness is eviscerated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:33.280 --> 01:01:41.440
And that's painful. It can be so painful that many&nbsp;
people don't come back from it, and I credit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:41.440 --> 01:01:45.760
10 years at that point, or nine years of therapy&nbsp;
I did at that point, plus the support of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:45.760 --> 01:01:51.360
therapist, the coach that I had, to surviving it.
But if you can survive it, what you end up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:51.360 --> 01:01:58.400
on the other side is all of those insecure, a lot&nbsp;
of the insecurities and all that ego bullshit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:58.400 --> 01:02:06.320
made you super ineffective, jealous, or triggered&nbsp;
for all sorts of different reasons, it's gone. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:06.320 --> 01:02:12.320
your image that you are this perfect, brilliant&nbsp;
leader that all successful founders form when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:19.680
are successful had to die. And the reason that's&nbsp;
so good is that that's so limiting. When you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:19.680 --> 01:02:28.320
this ego identity of yourself about how fucking&nbsp;
amazing you are, then any moment that challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:28.320 --> 01:02:36.800
that is super scary. Anyone who questions it is&nbsp;
offensive. And so I credit wherever I am today and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:36.800 --> 01:02:43.840
I have decades of learning still to go to those&nbsp;
three components. And I feel super fortunate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:43.840 --> 01:02:51.920
have had all of them, even though the last one&nbsp;
sucked, I can finally say, wow, it really helped.

01:02:51.920 --> 01:02:56.240
Thank you for sharing all that. I'm glad I&nbsp;
went there. I want to show you something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:56.240 --> 01:02:59.120
I randomly have in my office, my wife&nbsp;
just got me that I think you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:59.120 --> 01:03:02.400
to love. It's a piece of art that I think&nbsp;
will resonate with [inaudible 01:03:02].

01:03:02.400 --> 01:03:04.320
Yeah. What am I looking at here? So, it's a hand?

01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:08.480
It's a hand with a snap and then let&nbsp;
me see if you can see what it says.

01:03:08.480 --> 01:03:10.000
I can't see what it says.

01:03:10.000 --> 01:03:12.400
It says ego death now.

01:03:12.400 --> 01:03:15.600
Right. Look at this. Good. Exactly.

01:03:15.600 --> 01:03:16.240
There it is.

01:03:16.240 --> 01:03:23.600
May all our egos peacefully become smaller&nbsp;
and leave this mortal coil. The reality is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:23.600 --> 01:03:31.440
none of our egos ever die. And even great ...&nbsp;
Ram Dass is this great spiritual teacher who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:31.440 --> 01:03:38.880
died a few years ago and someone asked him&nbsp;
on his deathbed something like, "How did&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:38.880 --> 01:03:45.600
you get over your bullshit or your ego?" And he&nbsp;
said, "I never did. Just the edges got smoothed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:45.600 --> 01:03:54.720
away." And this is a guy who had 70 years of the&nbsp;
deepest, wildest spiritual work, he acknowledged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:54.720 --> 01:03:59.920
no, still my same self. So, the ego is still&nbsp;
there and we actually need to acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:59.920 --> 01:04:04.160
it and love it. And when you acknowledge&nbsp;
it, then it's not a surprise when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:09.120
like a little jealous and you're like, huh, I'm&nbsp;
jealous. That's funny. Okay. And it's all good.

01:04:09.120 --> 01:04:13.920
Reminds me Daniel Kahneman who wrote all these&nbsp;
books about biases that we have and here's all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:13.920 --> 01:04:18.720
the ways we're flawed. If people ask him, "Have&nbsp;
you learned to live more rationally?" He's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:18.720 --> 01:04:21.840
"Not at all." Knowing all these things&nbsp;
about how we're flawed in the way we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:21.840 --> 01:04:24.880
think all these biases doesn't&nbsp;
actually, I can't use it in life.

01:04:24.880 --> 01:04:30.160
We're human. We should let ourselves be human.&nbsp;
I think it's beautiful. We're logic systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:30.160 --> 01:04:35.600
but we're also heart systems and body systems&nbsp;
and soul systems. So, all of it is good.

01:04:35.600 --> 01:04:38.800
Okay, I want to go in a completely different&nbsp;
direction. The last thing I want to talk about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:38.800 --> 01:04:43.280
I needed to mention this. I don't know if you've&nbsp;
seen this, but I've been doing research on which&nbsp;&nbsp;

