become the first product manager at a future&nbsp;
startup, start their own companies. Intercom&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:05.440
is coming number one across this research&nbsp;
next to Palantir and Stripe, Revolut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:10.560
So, the question this begs is, what are you&nbsp;
guys doing that produces such great product&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:10.560 --> 01:05:15.360
leaders? There's the hiring piece and then&nbsp;
there's what they do at Intercom piece. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:15.360 --> 01:05:20.800
what do you think is creating these sort of&nbsp;
really big successes from your alumni group?

01:05:20.800 --> 01:05:26.960
Yeah, I don't have a really succinct answer&nbsp;
unfortunately. I can say in the abstract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:26.960 --> 01:05:34.800
our culture is a very producty culture. So,&nbsp;
myself and Des, there was four founders and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.800 --> 01:05:42.960
me and Des Traynor drove a lot, like all the&nbsp;
strategy. We're product guys. I was a software&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:42.960 --> 01:05:48.720
designer. I studied computer science, so I'm&nbsp;
technical, but never did it professionally. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.720 --> 01:05:54.400
the first part is that just product innovation,&nbsp;
design just was just core to our culture and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:54.400 --> 01:06:00.080
people always picked up on that. So, I think good&nbsp;
people wanted to work here and we were good at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.080 --> 01:06:07.040
finding good people. The other part was&nbsp;
that because we had this sprawling strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.040 --> 01:06:16.480
we had all these products that we needed a complex&nbsp;
structure for it and that included lots of PMs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:16.480 --> 01:06:24.800
PM groups that we gave a lot of autonomy to. And&nbsp;
so the product of our big messy strategy was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:24.800 --> 01:06:31.200
we had PMs that got to act like mini CEOs.
